program delivery_hour_limits

-- ============================================================
-- Delivery-hour limits: a position cap that holds in every hour
-- ============================================================
--
-- A power trading desk buys and sells electricity for delivery
-- later: this many megawatts, from this instant to that one. A
-- "block" is one trade covering several consecutive hours, a "day"
-- product covers a whole local calendar day. Risk gives each book a
-- cap: the book's net position, buys less sells, may not exceed the
-- cap in any delivery hour. Not on average over the trade, not in
-- total across the day - in each hour, because each hour is settled
-- on its own.
--
-- That last phrase is the whole difficulty. A trade is one claim with
-- two instants on it. The rule has to look at every hour the trade
-- touches, add up what the book already holds in that hour, and ask
-- whether this trade still fits. Adding up every trade that OVERLAPS
-- the new one is wrong: two trades that never share an hour would be
-- counted together, and a trade that fits would be refused. The grid
-- of hours is the thing the rule has to range over.
--
-- The record builds that grid itself. `periods(starts_at, ends_at,
-- duration(PT1H))` is the start of every hour the window touches,
-- hours counted on the hour from a fixed epoch, so two trades agree on
-- where an hour begins without either saying so. The caller passes
-- the trade and nothing else: no list of hours to be checked against
-- the trade, because the grid is computed from the trade.
--
-- One honesty the record demands in return: before it builds a grid
-- it is told how long a window may be. A rule says `require ends_at -
-- starts_at no_longer_than duration(PT744H)` and only then asks for
-- the hours. That is a business rule - a product is at most a month -
-- and it is also what keeps the size of the work the rule's own,
-- whatever a caller sends.

-- A book's cap, in megawatts, on the net position in any one hour.
predicate Limit(book: Subject, cap_mw: Decimal)
    unique by (book)

-- A captured trade: signed megawatts (bought positive, sold negative)
-- delivered over a half-open window of UTC instants.
predicate Trade(book: Subject, trade: Subject, mw: Decimal, starts_at: Timestamp, ends_at: Timestamp)
    unique by (trade)

-- A local calendar day, as a calendar authority publishes it: which
-- instants it runs between, under which version of the time-zone
-- database. The record does not compute this. Clocks change on days
-- the law decides, and the law changes; so the day's bounds are a
-- claim, with their authority and version on them, and the rules below
-- read the claim. A spring-forward day is 23 hours long, a fall-back
-- day 25, and nothing here needs to know which is which.
predicate DeliveryDay(calendar: Subject, local_date: Date, starts_at: Timestamp, ends_at: Timestamp, tzdb_version: Subject)
    unique by (calendar, local_date)

-- Who may publish a calendar's days.
predicate CalendarPublisher(calendar: Subject, publisher: Subject)
    unique by (calendar)

-- A cap is a positive figure.
invariant limits_are_positive:
    Limit(book, cap_mw) implies 0 < cap_mw

-- A published day is a civil day: exactly 23, 24 or 25 hours long. A
-- record of any other length is a publishing mistake, refused before
-- any trade can be captured against it.
invariant a_delivery_day_is_a_civil_day:
    let length = (ends_at - starts_at)
    DeliveryDay(calendar, local_date, starts_at, ends_at, _)
    implies (length = duration(PT23H) or length = duration(PT24H) or length = duration(PT25H))

-- The acts.

transformation set_limit(book, cap_mw):
    require not Limit(book, _)
    admit Limit(book, cap_mw)

-- The first act names the calendar's publisher; from then on only the
-- publisher's login can add days.
transformation appoint_calendar_publisher(calendar, publisher):
    require not CalendarPublisher(calendar, _)
    admit CalendarPublisher(calendar, publisher)

transformation publish_delivery_day(calendar, local_date, starts_at, ends_at, tzdb_version):
    require CalendarPublisher(calendar, actor)
    require not DeliveryDay(calendar, local_date, _, _, _)
    admit DeliveryDay(calendar, local_date, starts_at, ends_at, tzdb_version)

-- Capturing a block. The gates run in order, and the order matters.
-- First the window: forward, at most a month. Then the grid, built
-- from the window. Then alignment: a product runs from an hour
-- boundary to an hour boundary, so the hours it touches are exactly
-- as many as its length holds; a trade starting at half past touches
-- one hour more than it is long, and is refused. Only then the limit,
-- hour by hour: what the book already holds in that hour, counting a
-- trade when it covers the hour's start, plus this trade, within the
-- cap either way round. The refusal names the hour.
--
-- This is a gate, not an invariant, on purpose. A pre-trade limit is
-- checked when the trade is captured; lowering a cap later does not
-- unwind trades, it raises a breach to be managed, which is another
-- act and another record.
transformation capture(book, trade, mw, starts_at, ends_at):
    bind Limit(book, cap_mw)
    require not Trade(trade: trade, ..)
    require starts_at strictly_before ends_at
    require ends_at - starts_at no_longer_than duration(PT744H)
    let hours = periods(starts_at, ends_at, duration(PT1H))
    require whole_hours: (ends_at - starts_at) / duration(PT1H) = sum(1 | h in hours)
    require hour_within_limit: forall h in hours: abs(sum(m | Trade(book, _, m, s, e) and s at_or_before h and h strictly_before e) + mw) <= cap_mw
    admit Trade(book, trade, mw, starts_at, ends_at)

-- Capturing a day product. The caller names the calendar and the
-- local date; the window is the published day's, so the caller cannot
-- state it, and a day the authority never published cannot be traded.
-- The same hour-by-hour limit then runs over 23, 24 or 25 hours,
-- whichever the day has.
transformation capture_day(book, trade, mw, calendar, local_date):
    bind Limit(book, cap_mw)
    bind DeliveryDay(calendar, local_date, starts_at, ends_at, _)
    require not Trade(trade: trade, ..)
    require ends_at - starts_at no_longer_than duration(PT25H)
    let hours = periods(starts_at, ends_at, duration(PT1H))
    require whole_hours: (ends_at - starts_at) / duration(PT1H) = sum(1 | h in hours)
    require hour_within_limit: forall h in hours: abs(sum(m | Trade(book, _, m, s, e) and s at_or_before h and h strictly_before e) + mw) <= cap_mw
    admit Trade(book, trade, mw, starts_at, ends_at)

-- What this example deliberately does not cover: quarter-hour
-- products (the same grid with a 15-minute step), a cap that changes
-- over time, the breach raised when a cap is lowered under open
-- trades, and where the published days come from. The calendar
-- authority's claims are trusted as claims; the record governs what
-- is done with them, not whether the authority read its clock right.
